Introduction
Autoimmune encephalitis manifests acutely or subacutely
through seizures, and depending on whether it involves the
limbic system or not, through cognitive and/or neuropsychi-
atric disorders.
1 It is also classi ﬁed as limbic or extralimbic
encephalitis.1 The limbic forms are rare, particularly in
children, and they have variable symptoms and are therefore
probably under-diagnosed. Their diagnosis has improved
considerably over the past 10 years and involves a combina-
tion of radiological and clinical signs and the identi ﬁcation of
autoantibodies. These autoantibodies are separated into
different groups based on their target: cytoplasmic and
nuclear, intracellular synaptic, and membrane-bound.
1
Antiglutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies are well de-
scribed in type 1 diabetes 2 and are a known cause of non-
paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis, 2 with glutamic acid
decarboxylase (GAD) being an intracellular enzyme respon-
sible for synthesis of the gamma-aminobutyric acid neuro-
transmitter.
2 But the spectrum of neurologic disorders
associated with GAD antibodies is broad, including the
pediatric population, and are all characterized by the pres-
ence of high levels of GAD antibodies in the blood and
