Discussion
<seg id="s0005" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">This case associated with the diagnosis of GAD encephalitis
and SPS is, to our knowledge, the ﬁrst case described in a
child. This illustrates the variability of neurological syn-
dromes associated with these antibodies, 2 including in chil-
dren, despite the few cases known to date.</seg>
SPS3 is deﬁned by progressive muscle stiffness resulting in
progressive immobility of the trunk and hips. There is a clinical
variant that affects only a single limb (stiff limb syndrome) and
progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus,
which is associated with stiffness, myoclonus, and focal neuro-
logical signs. The diagnosis is clinical and is strengthened by the
identiﬁcation of GAD antibodies and on electromyographical
grounds. The treatment of this disease involves the use of
symptomatic treatments (benzodiazepines and baclofen) and
immunomodulatory treatments (corticosteroids, intravenous
immunoglobulin, plasmapheresis).
SPS is very rare in pediatrics, but it is possible and is
probably under-diagnosed.
4 The diagnosis is sometimes
retrospective in adults, attesting to its unfamiliarity in
children. In this American study
4 describing eight patients
with SPS whose symptoms began in childhood, each were
positive for antibodies and had a personal or family history of
autoimmunity. In adults, the presence of antibodies is part of
the diagnostic criteria, but the relation with the pathophysi-
ology has not been clearly established.
3
In the 12 published cases of GAD-positive encephalitis in
children (►Table 1), the majority were prepubescent and were
girls. Seizures were the most common clinical sign in these
children, whereas memory disorders were only described in
two cases. 5 The onset of symptoms is acute or subacute,
sometimes with a rather long time to treatment, re ﬂecting
possible diagnostic difﬁculties due to the absence of a typical
presentation. There is often an autoimmune comorbidity6–8 or
a disease conducive to autoimmunity. 9,10 Abnormalities are
usually found on MRI (white matter hyperintensities in T2-
weighted sequences), and subsequent assessments show atro-
phic sequelae. These abnormalities are generally multifocal
and most often include temporal involvement. Some children
have MRIs described as normal, which may be related to the
performance of the exam relative to the history of the disease.
All these children received immunomodulatory treatment
via courses of corticosteroids and/or intravenous
immunoglobulins.
5–14 In one case, 7 mycophenolate mofetil
was used when switching from oral maintenance corticosteroid
therapy due to poor tolerability and did not lead to clinical
improvement. Rituximab was used in several cases,
5,7,11 rather
as second-line treatment. Certain patients had plasma
exchanges6,7,10,13as ﬁrst- or second-line treatment. The review
of these cases does not appear to identify the most effective
strategy. Of these 12 children, 8 seemed to progress favorably, 4
had sequelae, and 1 died of severe autonomic disorders.
Finally, this case showed us that in response to rare
neurological symptoms in a child such as amnesia and SPS,
and when the clinical presentation meets the criteria for
deﬁning autoimmune encephalitis,
1 the etiological assess-
ment should be standardized, particularly concerning auto-
antibodies, and should include testing for GAD antibodies,
regardless of age. The association in the same patient of
encephalitis and SPS in this case reinforces the idea that GAD
autoantibodies are involved in the pathogenesis of these
neurological disorders, when no other antibodies were iden-
tiﬁed, despite unresolved pathophysiological components.
